window.docContents[220].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___12-Generalized-Logarithms-and-Exponentials/#chapter-4-section-12":{"contents":"Source: NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §4.12.\n\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions","header":"4.12. §4.12 Generalized Logarithms and Exponentials","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___12-Generalized-Logarithms-and-Exponentials/#chapter-4-section-12"},"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___10-Other-Series-Expansions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals--___6___10-Other-Series-Expansions--___6___10-Other-Series-Expansions":{"contents":"The first subsection gives an inverse-factorial expansion for the exponential\nintegral.  The coefficients are fixed by their initial values and a finite\nrecurrence.  The second subsection records expansions in spherical and\nmodified spherical Bessel functions; the real formula for Ei\nis stated away from its logarithmic singularity at zero.\n\nThe Lean disclosures use the canonical §6.2 relations for E1, Ei, Si, Cin,\nand Ein.  The inverse-factorial coefficients are an\nexecutable finite-prefix construction.  Spherical and modified spherical\nBessel functions are represented by normalized families whose initial values\nand recurrences identify the DLMF functions; this is the reusable interface\nneeded because Mathlib does not currently provide these two families as named\nmaps.\n\nThe inverse-factorial subsection introduces the coefficient family used in\nE1--E3; the first three displays below give its expansion, initial values, and\nfinite recurrence.\n\nThe source note cites Nielsen (1906a, p. 283).  It also records that the\nrecurrence in E3 follows from the generating function\n1/(1-ln(1-t)) = Σₖ cₖ tᵏ, and points to the related result in §6.18(i).\nFor the more general incomplete-gamma and logarithmic-integral results, the\nsame source note warns that Nielsen's Formula (3) is incorrect.\n\nThe generating-function specification fixes the same coefficients used by the\nexecutable finite-prefix construction, so the recurrence is not an arbitrary\nsequence assumption.\n\nFor complex z with positive real part, the inverse-factorial series is\\operatorname{E}_{1}(z)=e^{-z}\\left(\\frac{c_{0}}{z}+\\frac{c_{1}}{z(z+1)}+\\frac{2!c_{2}}{z(z+1)(z+2)}+\\frac{3!c_{3}}{z(z+1)(z+2)(z+3)}+\\cdots\\right).$$\n\nThe coefficient stream is executable: inverseFactorialPrefix (n + 1) stores\nthe first (n+1) coefficients, and inverseFactorialCoeff n reads its last\nentry.  The next entry is computed by the finite sum in the recurrence, so\nthe API does not quantify over an arbitrary sequence.\n\n\n\n\n\nThe first coefficients arec_{0}=1,\\qquad c_{1}=-1,\\qquad c_{2}=\\frac{1}{2},\\qquad c_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3},\\qquad c_{4}=\\frac{1}{6}.$$\n\nThe subsequent coefficients satisfyc_{k}=-\\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\\frac{c_{j}}{k-j},\\qquad k\\geq 1.$$\n\nThe next subsection uses the standard spherical-Bessel notation of §10.47(ii).\n\nFor the notation in E4--E8, consult the DLMF definition of spherical and\nmodified spherical Bessel functions in §10.47(ii).\nThe series statements below expose convergence with HasSum and then state\nthe corresponding value identity, rather than relying on a totalized infinite\nsum.\n\nThe normalized spherical-Bessel interface records the two initial values and\nthe three-term recurrence, including the nonzero argument condition.  The\nmodified-family recurrence is recorded in\n§10.51(ii), while the notation and\nnormalization are given in §10.47(ii).\nThus a formula below cannot silently substitute an unrelated spherical-Bessel\nsequence.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe sine integral has the spherical-Bessel expansion\\operatorname{Si}(z)=z\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\left(\\mathsf{j}_{n}\\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}z\\right)\\right)^{2}.$$\n\nThe cosine integral is expanded as\\operatorname{Cin}(z)=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}a_{n}\\left(\\mathsf{j}_{n}\\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}z\\right)\\right)^{2}.$$\n\nFor real x≠0, the modified spherical-Bessel expansion is stated directly for\nthe canonical §6.2 map realEi:\\operatorname{Ei}(x)=\\gamma+\\ln|x|+\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}(-1)^{n}(x-a_{n})\\left({\\mathsf{i}^{(1)}_{n}}\\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}x\\right)\\right)^{2}.$$\n\nThe coefficients in the preceding expansion area_{n}=(2n+1)\\left(1-(-1)^{n}+\\psi(n+1)-\\psi(1)\\right).$$\n\nFinally,\\operatorname{Ein}(z)=ze^{-z/2}\\left({\\mathsf{i}^{(1)}_{0}}\\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}z\\right)+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{2n+1}{n(n+1)}{\\mathsf{i}^{(1)}_{n}}\\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}z\\right)\\right).$$\n\nFor E4--E8 and further results, the source cites Harris (2000) and Luke\n(1969b, pp. 56--57).  It also notes that an expansion for E₁(z) follows by\ncombining §6.2.E4 with E8, and points to the\nrelated expansions in §6.18(i).\n\nend LMLFManual.Chapter6\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Exponential, Logarithmic, Sine, and Cosine Integrals\u0009§6.10 Other Series Expansions","header":"§6.10 Other Series Expansions","id":"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___10-Other-Series-Expansions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals--___6___10-Other-Series-Expansions--___6___10-Other-Series-Expansions"},"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___2-Definitions-and-Interrelations/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals--___6___2-Definitions-and-Interrelations--___6___2-Definitions-and-Interrelations--Principal-exponential-integrals":{"contents":"This group defines E₁, relates it to Ein, and records the real\nprincipal-value formulas for Ei and li. The path and limit\nvocabulary remains visible because DLMF's complex integral is not an arbitrary\ninterval integral.\n\n E_1(z)=\\int_z^\\infty \\frac{e^{-t}}{t}\\,dt,\\qquad z\\ne0.\nDLMF defines the principal E₁(z) for z ≠ 0 by integrating\nexp (-t) / t to infinity along an admissible path avoiding the cut and\nthe origin.\n\n E_1(z)=e^{-z}\\int_0^\\infty \\frac{e^{-t}}{t+z}\\,dt,\\qquad |\\operatorname{ph}z|<\\pi.\nOn the open principal-cut plane, the same value is the ray integral\nexp (-z) ∫₀^∞ exp (-t)/(t+z) dt.\n\n \\operatorname{Ein}(z)=\\int_0^z \\frac{1-e^{-t}}{t}\\,dt.\nEin(z) is entire and is represented by integrating\n(1-exp(-t))/t from zero to z, with its removable value at zero.\n\n E_1(z)=\\operatorname{Ein}(z)-\\ln z-\\gamma.\nOn the principal branch, E₁(z) = Ein(z) - log z - γ.\n\n \\operatorname{Ei}(x)=-\\operatorname{PV}\\int_{-x}^{\\infty}\\frac{e^{-t}}{t}\\,dt=\\operatorname{PV}\\int_{-\\infty}^{x}\\frac{e^t}{t}\\,dt,\\qquad x>0.\nFor x > 0, DLMF gives both principal-value integrals for Ei(x),\nwith the first on the negative ray and the second on the real line.\n\n \\operatorname{Ei}(-x)=-\\int_x^\\infty\\frac{e^{-t}}{t}\\,dt=-E_1(x),\\qquad x>0.\nFor positive x, the negative-axis value Ei(-x) is the negative of the\nordinary positive-ray integral ∫ₓ^∞ e⁻ᵗ/t dt, and hence equals -E₁(x).\nThe values are carried in ℂ so that the principal-value specifications and\nthe real identity share one codomain; this is the positive-real specialization\nof the source formula.\n\n \\operatorname{Ei}(\\pm x)=-\\operatorname{Ein}(\\mp x)+\\ln x+\\gamma,\\qquad x>0.\nFor x > 0, both Ei(±x) values are expressed through Ein,\nlog x, and Euler's constant.\nAs in E6, the real positive-axis values are represented in ℂ to retain the\nprincipal-value API.\n\n \\operatorname{li}(x)=\\operatorname{PV}\\int_0^x\\frac{dt}{\\ln t}=\\operatorname{Ei}(\\ln x),\\qquad x>1.\nFor x > 1, li(x) is the Cauchy principal value of\n∫₀ˣ dt/log t and agrees with Ei(log x). In the Lean specification,\nthe two finite-side integrability conditions are required only for the\nadmissible principal-value neighborhood 0 < ε < min 1 (x - 1); the displayed\ntruncation still converges as ε → 0⁺.\n\nDLMF's notation note places these exponential-integral symbols in the standard\nOlver notation system; the definitions retain the source's branch and\nprincipal-value conventions.\n\nThe path description is part of the complex definition: the path must not cross\nthe negative-real cut (-∞,0] or pass through the origin. On the negative-real\ncut (-∞,0), the principal value is two-valued; the formal maps here therefore\nstate the open-cut specialization.\n\nThe source places the E₁/Ein relation beside the ray representation; the Lean\nstatements preserve the distinction between an integral value and an\ninterrelation.\n\nThe real Ei formulas use x > 0, as in DLMF, and principal values at the\npole are written as symmetric limits.\n\nThe source says that Ei(x) is undefined at x = 0 and when x is not real;\nthe singular point is therefore excluded from the real Ei value specification.\nIts branch convention controls nonzero complex values.\n\nGeneralized E_p notation is developed later in DLMF §8.19; this page\nrecords only the §6.2 functions and their displayed interrelations.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Exponential, Logarithmic, Sine, and Cosine Integrals\u0009§6.2 Definitions and Interrelations\u0009§6.2 Definitions and Interrelations","header":"Principal exponential integrals","id":"/Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals/___6___2-Definitions-and-Interrelations/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Exponential___-Logarithmic___-Sine___-and-Cosine-Integrals--___6___2-Definitions-and-Interrelations--___6___2-Definitions-and-Interrelations--Principal-exponential-integrals"}});